# Ask on Ambiguity

When a request is underspecified in a way that would change the approach, scope, or outcome, ask before implementing. Do not silently guess on consequential choices.

## When to ask

Ask if any of these are unclear and would materially change the work:

- Goal or success criteria (what “done” means)
- Scope boundary (which files, crates, platforms, or surfaces)
- Approach when multiple valid options have different trade-offs
- Destructive or hard-to-reverse actions (deletes, renames, schema breaks, force push)
- Missing inputs that cannot be inferred from the repo or conversation

## When not to ask

- The ambiguity is trivial and a project convention or existing code already picks a default
- You can discover the answer quickly by reading the codebase
- The user already answered it earlier in the conversation
- Only cosmetic or naming details with an obvious local precedent

Prefer inferring from context and conventions over interrupting. Prefer one short clarifying round over guessing wrong and redoing work.

## How to ask

- Ask early, before large edits or irreversible steps
- Batch related questions in one message; do not drip-feed one question per turn
- Keep questions concrete and answerable (options or a yes/no when useful)
- If part of the request is clear, state what you will proceed with and only ask about the ambiguous part
- Do not pad with “just checking” questions that do not change the plan

## Examples

```
❌ User: “Fix the capture bug on Windows”
   Agent: Implements a speculative Win32 fix without confirming which bug or repro

✅ User: “Fix the capture bug on Windows”
   Agent: “Which failure are you seeing — black frames, wrong window, or focus not sticking? Any repro steps?”

❌ User: “Clean up the serializer”
   Agent: Rewrites the whole crate and renames public types

✅ User: “Clean up the serializer”
   Agent: “Do you mean dead-code removal and clippy cleanup, or a broader API reshape? Any types that must stay?”
```

## Relationship to concision

Stay concise: fewer, sharper questions. Asking on ambiguity overrides “pick one and proceed” only when the choice is consequential; otherwise still pick the conventional path and move.
